1
00:00:00,410 --> 00:00:03,541
It runs natively and in WebAssembly to classify,

2
00:00:03,541 --> 00:00:08,108
Tensile is one neural network kernel, written once in the Fe language.

3
00:00:08,108 --> 00:00:12,805
and on the Ethereum Virtual Machine to settle disputes about the answer.

4
00:00:13,159 --> 00:00:16,864
This page is the demo. There is no server and no wallet.

5
00:00:16,864 --> 00:00:24,274
This browser tab is running an Ethereum virtual machine, with the contracts exactly as Fe's compiler built them.

6
00:00:24,644 --> 00:00:25,881
Let's draw a seven.

7
00:00:31,061 --> 00:00:38,677
The network runs on the EVM, and gets it right. But look at the cost: about 1.9 million gas, for one answer.

8
00:00:38,677 --> 00:00:41,216
Nobody wants to pay that every time.

9
00:00:41,597 --> 00:00:48,104
So nobody runs it on-chain. A claimant just posts the answer, with every intermediate value and a bond.

10
00:00:48,463 --> 00:00:53,091
Now let's be dishonest. We'll claim this seven is a one. And not clumsily:

11
00:00:53,091 --> 00:00:59,471
we forge hidden neurons, then compute everything after them correctly, so every later step checks out.

12
00:00:59,867 --> 00:01:02,427
Posted, with a one ether bond of play money.

13
00:01:02,787 --> 00:01:06,904
A watcher re-runs the network off-chain and walks the claim in order.

14
00:01:06,904 --> 00:01:09,827
The first value that differs names the challenge.

15
00:01:10,195 --> 00:01:15,178
First, what if the watcher only challenges the final answer? The contract refuses.

16
00:01:15,178 --> 00:01:21,438
The forged outputs really are consistent with the forged neurons, so a later check cannot see this lie.

17
00:01:21,805 --> 00:01:25,183
The contract checks the vectors against the claim's hashes,

18
00:01:25,183 --> 00:01:26,557
Now the named challenge.

19
00:01:26,557 --> 00:01:31,938
proves the neuron's weights against the model's Merkle root, and re-runs just that one neuron.

20
00:01:33,090 --> 00:01:37,357
Half the bond now belongs to the watcher, and the other half is burned,

21
00:01:37,357 --> 00:01:40,963
Slashed. It took about 9 percent of the gas of the full run.

22
00:01:40,963 --> 00:01:44,930
so a lie costs its claimant even if the claimant slashes it first.

23
00:01:45,285 --> 00:01:50,277
An honest claim is different. Every challenge is refused, with the contract's own error.

24
00:01:53,534 --> 00:02:00,510
Once the hour passes, it becomes final. Only then does the result read back, and each account pulls its own money.

25
00:02:03,883 --> 00:02:07,436
Why Fe? Shapes and fixed-point scales live in the types.

26
00:02:07,436 --> 00:02:12,005
Layers of the wrong shape, or a bias at the wrong scale, do not compile.

27
00:02:12,005 --> 00:02:16,192
A model that could overflow on any input fails a static assertion.

28
00:02:16,791 --> 00:02:21,651
Effects are in the signatures: only withdraw declares that it can send ether.

29
00:02:21,651 --> 00:02:24,366
And all of this is nearly free at run time.

30
00:02:24,366 --> 00:02:30,615
The types cost about 2 percent of a neuron's execution gas, the pure state machine under 1 percent.

31
00:02:30,989 --> 00:02:31,932
The evidence.

32
00:02:31,932 --> 00:02:39,698
The NumPy reference and the native, WebAssembly and EVM builds agree bit for bit on all 10,000 test images.

33
00:02:39,698 --> 00:02:46,012
45 fuzz and invariant tests pass. All 59 deliberate faults in the contract were caught.

34
00:02:46,012 --> 00:02:52,834
And a hand-written Solidity twin passes the same suite: Fe costs 1.26 times its gas per claim.

35
00:02:53,192 --> 00:02:54,945
The limits are in the readme.

36
00:02:54,945 --> 00:03:00,871
It needs at least one honest watcher, the model is small, and nothing here is audited or deployed.

37
00:03:00,871 --> 00:03:04,136
Everything you saw can be rebuilt from the repository.

38
00:03:04,503 --> 00:03:07,276
Tensile. A false answer, caught by one neuron.
